WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.900
Florida Governor Ron

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.335
DeSantis is doing everything but

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.838
but announcing his run for 2024 out

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:05.905
with a new book

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.973
out with a new video

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.309
paid for by his campaign committee.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:10.744
Let's have a brief clip of that

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.679
and I want to get your reaction

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.815
We are the nation's fastest

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:17.250
growing state.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.886
We ranked number one in education

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:20.720
freedom.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.656
We are number one in economic freedom.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.225
Florida also ranks

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:28.194
number one in public higher education.

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:31.798
This is a record we can all be proud of.

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:33.433
For the state's governor, DeSantis

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:34.267
because he's a winner.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.568
That's what the guy does. He went.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:37.837
Decline is a choice.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.974
Success is attainable and freedom

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.177
is worth fighting for.

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:46.579
That looks a lot like a video

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:47.747
for a presidential candidate.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:48.948
Margaret Talev.

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:51.718
Did the music give you a clue?

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:52.619
Also,

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:54.721
this is the part of the silly part

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:57.590
of the campaign season where everyone

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.225
has the conversation that says,

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.660
does this mean that Ron

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.861
DeSantis is going to run?

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:02.796
And the answer is yes.

00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:03.329
Obviously,

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.098
why would you do any of these things

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.466
if you weren't intending to run?

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:08.835
The question is, what is the rollout?

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.403
What's the timing of that announcement?

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:11.237
How do you stage it?

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:12.639
And we can see some clues

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:13.840
from the ramp up

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.608
events around a book release

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.377
and around his decision

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:19.312
at this point not to participate in CPAC

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:20.113
because he doesn't have to.

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:21.247
He's going to get his own coverage

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.550
and differentiate himself from the pack.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.051
Laura?

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.853
Donald Trump clearly not comfortable

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:28.655
with a Ron DeSantis candidacy.

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:29.589
He's already

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:30.056
trying out

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:30.824
a bunch of nicknames

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:32.792
for him and attacking him

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.727
saying that he's responsible for his

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.963
for DeSantis, his success.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.364
How does that

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:39.632
potential rivalry

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.502
play out in any Republican primary?

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:42.769
I mean,

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:43.837
are other Republicans

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:44.771
do they see opportunity,

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.639
perhaps, that DeSantis and Trump

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:47.607
consume each other

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:51.478
Potentially.

00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:52.178
But I think that

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:53.613
a lot of the Republican strategists

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:54.047
I've talked to,

00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:55.415
you think that the more candidates

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:57.584
that jump in, the more that that benefit

00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:59.552
the former President Trump

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:01.955
in particular with DeSantis, mean

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.656
he is the biggest threat

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:04.791
to the former president

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:06.993
and the president.

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:08.261
The former president

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.762
does have some avenues

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.698
to try to differentiate himself

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:12.532
from DeSantis,

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:13.600
particularly on something

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.367
that we're hearing a lot

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:15.969
about from President Biden right now,

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.171
which is Medicare and Social Security.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:18.671
Trump did

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:20.473
this last time around, pointing out

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.041
that a lot of these Republicans

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:24.244
supported potential cuts to entitlement

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:25.612
and DeSantis was one of them.

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:26.279
And that's something

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:27.647
that I think we could see

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:32.018
the different little policy differences

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.087
that the former president tries to

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:36.623
to point out, particularly with DeSantis.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:40.160
Indeed, Laura Baron Lopez.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:40.960
Margaret Talev.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.629
Good to have you both on with me.

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:43.496
To share their reporting

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:44.397
and their insights,

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:46.399
Arianna Sotomayor of The Washington Post,

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:47.734
CNN's Dana Bash

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.936
and Francesca Chambers of USA Today.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.804
It is interesting, as others

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.173
get into the race,

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:54.240
DeSantis feels

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.074
he has the luxury

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:56.743
of doing a book tour first

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:57.610
and then getting in

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.546
because if you look at the early polling

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:01.447
emphasis on early polling,

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:02.882
he's up there with Donald Trump.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:03.983
Everybody else is way,

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:05.718
way, way, way, way behind.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.353
Yeah, he's definitely waiting the waters

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.788
and he can afford to do that right now.

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:09.722
There's still time,

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.124
even though we are in this world

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:12.992
of very early political races.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.061
The interesting thing about DeSantis is

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.262
there are a number of Republicans,

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:17.263
including on Capitol Hill,

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.398
who very much applauded

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:19.332
the fact that he

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:20.033
really blew it

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:21.868
out of the water in his governor's

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.036
reelection race.

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.170
But the thing is, is as

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.071
we saw in the midterms,

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:25.605
and this is going to be

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:26.239
an interesting thing

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.441
as he's going around the country

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:29.542
Pennsylvania,

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:32.612
Michigan, states that really were pivotal

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:34.681
to a lot of Republican voters,

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:35.515
actually said,

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:35.915
you know what,

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.383
these people who are running like Trump

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:38.518
who are just focusing

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:39.252
on the culture wars,

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:42.155
that is a staple of DeSantis as governor.

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:43.556
I have no idea yet

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:44.624
if that's going to resonate

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:45.525
in those places

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:47.493
that really do elect the president.

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.195
But I think that's a fascinating point

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:49.662
in the sense

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:50.997
that presidential primaries

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:52.031
tend to litigate

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:52.699
and they're supposed

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:54.267
to settle differences.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:56.135
When a party is debating who are we,

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:57.170
what are we for?

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.438
DeSantis is interesting.

00:03:58.438 --> 00:03:59.539
He's just signing into law

00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:00.740
now this new law in Florida

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:01.241
that allows him

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:02.809
essentially to control Disney,

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:04.277
to have some significant control

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:05.044
over Disney.

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.779
And listen to this in an interview

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.581
with Mark Levin on Fox over the weekend.

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.250
Again, he's promoting his new book

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:10.850
and he's talking

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.518
about using government power

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:13.453
against those who do

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.921
things with whom you disagree

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.090
that Wall Street

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:17.757
banks will

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:18.825
not give a loan

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:20.827
to someone, say, in the firearm industry

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.162
that's effectively changing

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:24.931
gun rights in America,

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.833
not getting any votes to do that.

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:28.167
They're not winning any elections

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:28.901
to do that,

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.570
but they're bringing power to bear

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.539
in a way that does affect public policy.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:36.776
So help me with the intellectual

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.578
consistency question.

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:40.713
Republicans say if you are a baker

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:42.782
and you don't want to sell a cake

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:45.485
or provide services to a gay wedding,

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:47.153
that should be your right

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:48.454
as an individual.

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:49.255
Ron DeSantis

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:51.557
there is saying if a bank decides

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.026
it will not lend money to somebody

00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:53.393
with whom

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:54.727
they have some disagreement,

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.129
the government can step in

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:57.997
and do something about it. Huh.

00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:00.466
It's such a fascinating

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.002
dichotomy slash contradiction

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:05.371
that we are going to see play out

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.674
as part of the Republican

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:09.375
nominating process.

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:10.376
And that is

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.478
what does being a conservative

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:13.246
actually mean?

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:16.883
Does it mean the historic definition,

00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:18.151
which is keep government

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:18.918
out of your life,

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:20.920
maybe more leaning towards a libertarian?

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:22.989
That is certainly what you're going to

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:23.556
you're going to hear

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.591
and see some of the candidates say

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:26.926
and you're going to hear them

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:29.262
say that they're the true conservative,

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:31.064
not the DeSantis

00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:33.032
wing or DeSantis himself,

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.335
who says, I'm going to enforce

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:36.903
the conservative ideals

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:38.538
by using the government.

00:05:38.538 --> 00:05:41.574
It's absolutely, as I said, fascinating.

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:43.009
I asked Ronna McDaniel,

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:44.444
the RNC chair, to sort of

00:05:44.444 --> 00:05:45.244
try to litigate that.

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:46.579
Of course, she wouldn't,

00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:47.880
because we do see that

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:48.781
coming down the pike

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:49.949
as one of the key questions

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:51.351
you see coming down the pike.

00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:52.085
The Club for Growth

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:53.753
is one of the conservative groups

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:54.854
that are calling people together

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:56.255
essentially cattle calls.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:57.156
They bring donors in

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:58.324
and they bring potential candidates.

00:05:58.324 --> 00:05:59.492
And so people can try to impress,

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:01.194
to raise money at the Club for Growth.

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:01.861
Guest list includes

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.162
Mike Pence, Nikki Haley, Governor

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:04.931
DeSantis, the Mike Pompeo

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:06.466
a Governor, Youngkin of Virginia, Senator

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:07.233
Tim Scott.

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:08.267
I mean, again,

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:09.669
you have this debate about

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:10.303
it used to be

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:11.371
Ronald Reagan's Republican

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:12.572
Party, to your point was keep

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:14.140
the government out as much as possible.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:14.941
Free market

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:16.075
Don't tell the private business

00:06:16.075 --> 00:06:16.743
what to do.

00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:17.677
Let them do their thing

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:19.712
and have basic regulation.

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:20.813
We're going to have a fascinating

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:21.414
debate over

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:22.715
DeSantis, who

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:25.151
a lot of Trump voters will like that

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.153
use your power to punish your enemies

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:28.421
or punish those who cross you.

00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:30.256
But it's not free market conservatism.

00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:31.057
But also notice

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:32.725
who was left off that list,

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:33.993
which is Donald Trump

00:06:33.993 --> 00:06:34.994
The Club for Growth tells me

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:37.230
he was not invited to this confab.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.998
So it does again show you

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:40.433
how there are many Republicans

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:42.168
who want to keep Trump's ideals,

00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:44.337
but they want to move on from Trumpism.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:45.938
But to your point, Dana.

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:47.273
But what does that even mean

00:06:47.273 --> 00:06:47.840
at this point?

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.108
Several of the Republican

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:49.942
candidates are Would-Be

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:51.511
candidates have really just struggled

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:53.045
to define in the last couple of weeks

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.847
how their policies would be different

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:55.515
than Trump, how

00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:57.417
they would actually be different

00:06:57.417 --> 00:06:58.117
than Donald Trump.

00:06:58.117 --> 00:06:58.985
So I do think

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:00.386
that that is going to be a key

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:01.654
question that's facing

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:02.688
many of these candidates

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:04.223
is beyond January six,

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:04.590
like what

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:05.458
would you actually do

00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:06.692
that's different than Donald Trump

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:07.560
and why you versus

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.694
someone else on this stage?

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:10.229
And when we get them on a stage,

00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:11.831
thank you for the use of that term.

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:13.299
August, the first planned

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:15.067
Republican presidential primary debate.

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:16.202
You mentioned your conversation

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:17.503
with Chairwoman McDaniel.

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:18.738
She was appointed by Trump.

00:07:18.738 --> 00:07:19.806
She's viewed by many in the party.

00:07:19.806 --> 00:07:20.473
You don't like Trump

00:07:20.473 --> 00:07:21.574
as too close to Trump.

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:22.508
She's clearly deciding

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.609
to create some space

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:24.610
or try to create some space

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:25.111
for Donald Trump,

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.179
including the idea

00:07:26.179 --> 00:07:26.546
she says,

00:07:26.546 --> 00:07:28.614
if you want to be on the stage of a party

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:29.849
sponsored debate,

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:31.684
you should sign a pledge promising

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:32.852
that if you're not the nominee,

00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:34.086
you will support who is.

00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:36.722
If you're going to be on the Republican

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:38.558
National Committee debate stage

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:40.026
asking voters to support you,

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:40.993
you should say I'm going

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:41.961
to support the voters

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:42.795
and who they choose

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:45.031
as the nominee, as RNC chair.

00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:46.532
If I said I wouldn't

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:48.301
support the Republican nominee,

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:50.136
I would be removed from office.

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:51.671
Anybody getting on the Republican

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:53.439
National Committee debate stage

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:54.540
should be able to say

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.042
I will support the will of the voters

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:58.411
and the eventual nominee of our party.

00:07:58.411 --> 00:07:59.345
It worked

00:07:59.345 --> 00:08:00.880
at least to a degree, in the sense

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:01.614
that within an hour

00:08:01.614 --> 00:08:03.416
or so after that interview,

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:04.217
Trump's campaign

00:08:04.217 --> 00:08:05.585
said, of course, released a

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:06.452
and of course, we'll support

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:07.053
the Republican nominee

00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:08.521
because it will be me. Right.

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:09.422
You know, so

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:11.624
but but in a way, she at least

00:08:11.624 --> 00:08:12.658
got him halfway there.

00:08:13.626 --> 00:08:14.427
Yes.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:15.661
Obviously, Donald

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.063
Trump and the question about him

00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:18.498
and his loyalty

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:21.367
is one of the factors in the equation.

00:08:21.868 --> 00:08:22.935
It hasn't been tested

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:25.438
because the first time Republicans signed

00:08:25.438 --> 00:08:28.374
this was in 2016 because of the

00:08:29.075 --> 00:08:30.910
sort of consternation about Donald Trump

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:32.245
as the potential nominee.

00:08:32.245 --> 00:08:32.812
Wasn't tested

00:08:32.812 --> 00:08:34.313
because he became the nominee.

00:08:34.313 --> 00:08:37.817
Now it's it's so multifaceted

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.119
because it's not just about Donald Trump

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:41.587
and who he support.

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.055
It's even more importantly,

00:08:43.055 --> 00:08:45.291
the other candidates, especially those

00:08:45.558 --> 00:08:46.492
who have said

00:08:46.492 --> 00:08:48.361
very explicitly Donald Trump

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:49.929
should not be president again

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:50.997
he is not qualified.

00:08:50.997 --> 00:08:52.965
He tried to overthrow the government.

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:54.467
How can I sign a pledge

00:08:54.467 --> 00:08:55.968
supporting his candidacy?

00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:58.137
You're asking me to put

00:08:58.137 --> 00:08:59.071
party over country

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:00.706
and I don't feel comfortable with that.

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:03.242
That is one of the open questions

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:04.610
about what those candidates will do.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:05.278
And so do

00:09:05.278 --> 00:09:07.179
they want people to actually sign pen

00:09:07.179 --> 00:09:08.481
to paper, all of them?

00:09:08.481 --> 00:09:10.049
Well, and some Republicans

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:10.616
tell me that

00:09:10.616 --> 00:09:12.451
they don't see this as enforceable.

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:12.752
Sure.

00:09:12.752 --> 00:09:14.820
You sign it, you get on the debate stage.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:15.421
But after that,

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:16.422
if you're not the nominee,

00:09:16.422 --> 00:09:17.523
what's to actually make

00:09:17.523 --> 00:09:19.592
you support Donald Trump?

00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:21.360
Of course, you're your own truthfulness.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.262
Your own conscious, of course, is

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:25.197
what one of the Republican

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:26.098
potential candidates,

00:09:26.098 --> 00:09:27.466
Asa Hutchinson told me is,

00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:28.801
is that you have your own word there

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:29.468
that you have to look at.

00:09:29.468 --> 00:09:31.904
But they see it as a potential impediment

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:33.606
for getting more people on

00:09:33.606 --> 00:09:34.507
the debate stage

00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:35.641
is having to write a quote

00:09:35.641 --> 00:09:37.176
unquote blank check, as he called it,

00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:38.911
to support the eventual nominee.

00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:40.079
So I'm trying to think

00:09:40.079 --> 00:09:41.280
who would be in the group of people

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:42.915
who would not sign such a thing?

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:43.950
Liz Cheney,

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.151
maybe Larry Hogan,

00:09:45.151 --> 00:09:46.352
who might run anyone else?

00:09:46.352 --> 00:09:48.154
Chris Christie. Chris Christie.

00:09:48.154 --> 00:09:49.522
All right. We'll see how that list grows.

